{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>You shall not add to the word that I command you, nor shall you diminish from it.</b> He warns that a person should not be clever concerning the commandments, saying 'I will add to what HaShem, blessed be He, has commanded,' thinking that this addition will be service to HaShem, blessed be He. Therefore it says לא תוסיפו — 'you shall not add' — for the Torah is complete; it needs neither addition nor diminution, and whoever adds to it diminishes. And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted this 'addition' as referring to such cases as the four passages in tefillin, where one might say 'I will add one more and make them five'; the four species of the lulav, where one might add and make them five; and similarly the Priestly Blessing, which is three, where one might add and make it four. But what our Rabbis of blessed memory enacted — their fences and safeguards — is not called 'addition,' for they came only to guard the essence, just as one makes a fence around a vineyard so that the vineyard is protected. As the saying goes: 'To you, to you, they say to the Nazirite — go around, go around; to the vineyard do not come near.' On the contrary, one who violates their fences and enactments transgresses the negative commandment (Deuteronomy 17:11), דלא תסור מן הדבר — 'that you shall not turn aside from the matter'; and so the Torah commanded (Leviticus 18:30): ושמרתם את משמרתי — 'and you shall keep My charge,' and our Rabbis of blessed memory said: make a charge for My charge (עשו משמרת למשמרתי)."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:45:13.390063283Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}